{"answerText":"No. Four bottle-filling stations with no drinking fountains are not permitted where four drinking fountains are required. In a nonrestaurant occupancy, only **up to 50 percent** of the required drinking fountains may be replaced by dedicated container-filling fixtures; those substitute fixtures must be adjacent to or readily visible from a drinking fountain conforming to PC § 410.1. Therefore, a four-fountain requirement can be met by at least **two conforming drinking fountains** plus no more than two qualifying bottle-filling fixtures—not four stand-alone bottle-filling stations. Bottled-water dispensers cannot be substituted. (PC § 410.3)

Further, each drinking fountain required by Table 403.1 must itself have both a bubbler faucet and a separate container-filling faucet for a container at least 10 inches high. (PC § 410.1) The stated four-fountain requirement is presumed to be the applicable Table 403.1 count; that table sets fixture minimums based on Building Code occupancy classification and occupant load. (PC § 403.1) The small-occupancy exemption does not alter this answer because the question establishes that four fountains are required; PC § 410.2 otherwise exempts occupancies with an occupant load of 15 or fewer.

For final design review, confirm that any proposed bottle-filling units are dedicated plumbing fixtures designed for a 10-inch-high container and that each is adjacent to or readily visible from one of the two retained conforming drinking fountains.","supportedPoints":[{"heading":"Bottle-filling fixtures may replace only half of required fountains","explanation":"In occupancies other than restaurants where water is served, up to 50 percent of required drinking fountains may be substituted with dedicated plumbing fixtures having faucets designed to fill containers at least 10 inches high. Each substitute fixture must be adjacent to or readily visible from a drinking fountain conforming to PC 410.1. Bottled-water dispensers are not permissible substitutes.","sectionID":"11961","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11961-1"]},{"heading":"Required fountains need both drinking and filling faucets","explanation":"Drinking fountains required by Table 403.1 must have both a bubbler faucet for drinking and a separate faucet designed to fill a container at least 10 inches high. The provision also specifies applicable product, water-quality, accessibility, and lead-compliance standards for drinking fountains and water coolers.","sectionID":"11959","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11959-2"]},{"heading":"The stated requirement is a Table 403.1 fixture minimum","explanation":"PC 403.1 requires plumbing fixtures in the minimum numbers shown in Table 403.1, with occupancy classification and occupant load determined under the New York City Building Code.","sectionID":"11909","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11909-4"]},{"heading":"Small occupancies need no fountains","explanation":"Drinking fountains are not required where the occupant load is 15 or fewer. This exemption does not change the answer because the question establishes that four drinking fountains are required.","sectionID":"11960","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11960-3"]}],"assumptions":[],"missingFacts":["For final design verification: whether each proposed bottle-filling station is a dedicated plumbing fixture with a faucet designed for a container at least 10 inches high.","For final design verification: whether each proposed substitute station will be adjacent to or readily visible from a retained drinking fountain that conforms to PC 410.1."],"followUpQuestions":[],"evidenceLimitations":["The supplied enacted evidence establishes the substitution limit and the listed fountain requirements, but does not provide product details or layout drawings needed to verify a particular station's design, adjacency, visibility, or full conformance with the standards referenced by PC 410.1."],"additionalEvidenceNeeded":["Manufacturer information for each proposed station, including its container-clearance capability and whether it is a dedicated plumbing fixture.","A floor plan showing the locations of the retained drinking fountains and proposed bottle-filling stations."] ,"supportingSourceUses":[],"citations":[{"sectionID":"11961","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11961-1"],"relevance":"Controls the 50-percent maximum substitution of required drinking fountains and conditions for a qualifying bottle-filling fixture."},{"sectionID":"11959","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11959-2"],"relevance":"Requires Table 403.1 drinking fountains to have both a bubbler and a separate qualifying container-filling faucet."},{"sectionID":"11909","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11909-4"],"relevance":"Establishes that Table 403.1 supplies minimum plumbing-fixture counts and that the Building Code determines occupancy classification and occupant load."},{"sectionID":"11960","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11960-3"],"relevance":"States the occupant-load-of-15-or-fewer exemption for drinking fountains."}]}
